Chinese Dynastic Cycle (Traditional Historiography – e.g., Sima Qian’s Shiji/Records of the Grand Historian, Book of Documents, official dynastic histories):  Mandate of Heaven: Virtuous founder rises → peak prosperity/population growth → moral corruption, overpopulation, natural disasters/famines → rebellions, loss of mandate → collapse and replacement.  
Phases: Rise (strong governance) → Peak (cultural/economic flourishing) → Decline (corruption, instability). Recurs across Xia/Shang/Zhou, Han, Tang, etc. Emphasizes moral + practical factors (e.g., ruler virtue, disasters triggering peasant revolts).  
Validates continuity of Chinese civilization through cycles rather than linear rupture. 

