Joseph Tainter (The Collapse of Complex Societies, 1988 – archaeological/historical synthesis with economic lens):  Collapse = rapid, significant loss of sociopolitical complexity (less stratification, specialization, central control, information flow, monumental investment, coordination).  
Core mechanism: Diminishing marginal returns on investments in complexity (energy subsidies, bureaucracy, problem-solving institutions). Societies add complexity to solve problems (e.g., resource shortages); initially high returns, then sharply declining benefits. Symptoms (invasion, drought, disease) become fatal when returns are low.  
Case studies with data:  Western Roman Empire: Agricultural/population pressures; conquest yielded diminishing resources vs. rising admin/military costs; fragmentation (some populations benefited from decentralization). Archaeological: urban shrinkage, estate decline.  
Classic Maya (lowlands): Investments in complexity (monuments, elites, agriculture) hit diminishing returns amid stresses.  
Chacoan (Ancestral Puebloans): Similar rapid devolution.

Broader: 17+ collapse examples; modern parallels in EROI (energy return on investment), education/tech returns. Collapse often rational/local preference for simpler systems when complexity costs exceed benefits. 

